update public.blog_posts set excerpt = $q$What makes a mechanical watch or clock tick — and why centuries-old ideas still sit at the heart of the timepieces collectors love.$q$, body = $q$Mechanical timekeeping is one of humanity's great achievements: a marriage of precision engineering, craftsmanship and scientific understanding that has been refined for roughly seven centuries.

## From tower clocks to the pocket

The first mechanical clocks appeared in the towers of Italian cities in the first half of the 14th century. They were driven by falling weights and regulated by a *verge-and-foliot* escapement, a simple mechanism whose rate depended heavily on the driving force and friction. By around 1500, makers had learned to replace the heavy weights with a coiled mainspring, which made smaller, portable clocks and the first watches possible.

The great leap in accuracy came in the 17th century. In 1656 the Dutch scientist Christiaan Huygens built the first pendulum clock, cutting errors from many minutes a day to under a minute. Around 1675 he applied the same principle to portable timepieces with the balance spring, the hairspring that still regulates mechanical watches today.

## The heart of the machine

Every mechanical timepiece shares the same basic architecture. A power source, a mainspring or weight, stores energy. A gear train carries that energy toward the hands. The escapement releases it in small, equal steps, and an oscillator, a pendulum or a balance wheel with its spring, sets the rhythm of those steps. The steady tick you hear is the escapement locking and unlocking, many times each second.

## Why it still matters

Why do people still treasure mechanical watches and clocks in an age of atomic time and smartphones? Part of the answer is the tangible connection to craftsmanship. A mechanical movement is built, finished and adjusted by skilled hands, and it can be serviced and kept running for generations.

For members of RIHOSO, the fascination is both intellectual and emotional. We gather not just to admire these machines but to understand them: to learn the language of horology and to appreciate the generations of innovation that brought us from tower clocks to wristwatch movements only a few millimetres thick.$q$, sources = $q$[{"title": "A Walk Through Time: A Revolution in Timekeeping \u2014 NIST", "url": "https://www.nist.gov/node/446006"}]$q$::jsonb where slug = 'art-of-mechanical-timekeeping';
update public.blog_posts set excerpt = $q$From colonial Newport workshops to Connecticut's first clock factories, New England turned clockmaking from a luxury craft into an American industry.$q$, body = $q$New England holds a special place in the history of American horology. From the colonial era through the early industrial age, the region's craftsmen turned clockmaking from an exclusive luxury into something ordinary households could afford.

## Colonial craftsmen, including Rhode Island's own

Before factories, clocks were made one at a time by individual craftsmen. Rhode Island was part of that story. William Claggett (1696–1749) worked in Newport, and one of his tall case clocks, made around 1730–1740, is in the collection of the RISD Museum in Providence.

In Connecticut, the English-trained Thomas Harland arrived in Norwich in 1773, repairing watches and making clock movements. His apprentice Daniel Burnap made tall clock movements in South Windsor, and Burnap in turn trained a young Eli Terry.

## Eli Terry and the Porter Contract

Terry set up shop in what is now Plymouth, Connecticut, making clocks with wooden and brass parts. By 1806 he had refined his wooden movement and was cutting gear teeth with water-powered saws. In 1807 he agreed to produce 4,000 tall case clock movements for Edward and Levi Porter of Waterbury within three years, an almost unthinkable number at the time.

To meet it, Terry redesigned his workshop and his methods, and he delivered. ConnecticutHistory.org describes the Porter Contract as the first successful large-scale production of a complicated mechanism with truly interchangeable parts, an early milestone of American industry. Terry went on to design a wooden movement small enough for a shelf or mantel, which cut the cost of both the movement and the case.

## A lasting legacy

Terry's apprentices and successors, including Seth Thomas and Silas Hoadley, helped turn Connecticut clockmaking into a full industry. Its traditions of precision manufacturing, apprenticeship and craftsmanship still shape American horology. Many of these clocks are still ticking in New England homes, and museums across the region preserve the story.$q$, sources = $q$[{"title": "Marking Time: Early Connecticut Innovations Transform Clock Making \u2014 ConnecticutHistory.org", "url": "https://connecticuthistory.org/?p=21579"}, {"title": "William Claggett, Tall Case Clock (ca. 1730\u20131740) \u2014 RISD Museum", "url": "https://risdmuseum.org/art-design/collection/tall-case-clock-1991177"}]$q$::jsonb where slug = 'new-england-clockmaking-heritage';
update public.blog_posts set excerpt = $q$A beginner's guide to building a meaningful watch collection: what to learn first, how to look, and why community matters.$q$, body = $q$Watch collecting is a deeply personal pursuit. There is no single right way to build a collection: it should reflect your interests, your curiosity and your connection to the art of timekeeping.

For beginners, the world of watches can seem overwhelming, with thousands of brands, countless complications and a vocabulary all its own. Every collector started somewhere, and the best advice is simple: learn first, then buy what speaks to you.

## Learn the basics

Start with a few key ideas. A **manual-wind** watch is powered by a mainspring you wind by hand; an **automatic** (self-winding) watch winds itself with a rotor that turns as you move. A **complication** is any function beyond telling the hours, minutes and seconds, such as a date, a chronograph or a moon-phase display. Learning the histories of the major watchmaking houses, and of the movements inside their watches, will help you understand what makes a piece interesting.

## Look, handle and ask

Handle watches whenever you can. Visit local watch shops and watch fairs, including RIHOSO's vintage watch shows, and talk with collectors who are happy to share what they know. Seeing many examples side by side teaches you more about condition, originality and proportion than any photograph.

## Buy carefully

Set a budget and stick to it. Interesting, historically significant watches exist at every level, and patience usually beats impulse. Buy from sellers who describe a watch honestly, including its service history and any replaced parts, and ask questions before you pay.

## Join a community

Most importantly, join a community. Organizations like RIHOSO give you the chance to learn from experienced collectors, see pieces you might never encounter on your own, and share the joy of horology with people who understand it.$q$, sources = $q$[]$q$::jsonb where slug = 'getting-started-with-watch-collecting';
update public.blog_posts set excerpt = $q$Bringing a silent antique clock back to life is part detective work, part engineering — and the first rule is to do no harm.$q$, body = $q$There is something deeply satisfying about bringing an antique clock back to life. A timepiece that has been silent for decades, perhaps centuries, begins to tick again, its pendulum swinging and its hands marking the hours as they were meant to.

## Detective work first

Clock restoration is equal parts detective work, engineering and artistry. It begins with careful examination: understanding the movement, identifying missing or damaged parts, and researching the clock's maker, period and original specifications. Thorough documentation, with photographs and notes taken before and during disassembly, makes every later decision easier.

## Do no harm

The first rule is to do no harm. Wherever possible, original parts should be preserved and repaired rather than replaced. Museum conservators go further: when the Cleveland Museum of Art and its partners treated an 18th-century French clock, they deliberately avoided replacing original components, and cleaned its brass mounts in a way that kept their patina rather than polishing it away. The gentle wear on brass and the darkening of steel tell the story of a clock's life.

## Cleaning and care

Cleaning is often the most important step. Years of dust, dried oil and corrosion can bring a movement to a standstill. Careful disassembly, appropriate cleaning and re-oiling with suitable lubricants can work wonders, but methods matter: the wrong chemical or an over-enthusiastic polish can do lasting damage. Valuable or unusual clocks are best entrusted to a trained clockmaker or conservator.

At RIHOSO we share techniques, discoveries and mistakes, because in restoration, as in horology itself, there is always more to learn.$q$, sources = $q$[{"title": "Many Hands of Time \u2014 Cleveland Museum of Art", "url": "https://www.clevelandart.org/articles/many-hands-time"}]$q$::jsonb where slug = 'restoring-antique-clocks';
